## Diagnosis of run 3's failure

`go test` itself reported the failure honestly (per task 032's fix, this is
the real `go test` exit status, never a `tee`-masked one): every package
except `features` was `ok`; `features` failed with exactly one scenario red
out of 164 run in that package:

```
--- FAIL: TestFeatures (144.68s)
    --- FAIL: TestFeatures/SIGKILL_captures_and_sanitizes_the_agent_pane_without_relaunching (5.98s)
        suite.go:640: after scenario hook failed: deck client "A" did not exit cleanly: hung deck client killed after 5s
```

`features/crash.feature`'s "SIGKILL captures and sanitizes the agent pane
without relaunching" scenario passed every one of its own assertions —
the reconcile-to-`error` transition, the sanitized 200-line crash artifact,
tmux session teardown, the audit launch record, and the detail dialog
showing "crash final line" all succeeded (visible in the frame dump: the
detail dialog is open showing the crash tail and "Pane is dead (signal 9,
...)" when the failure fires). The failure is entirely in the scenario's
*teardown* step, `deck client "A" exits cleanly`
(`features/assertions_test.go`'s `clientExitsCleanly`): it sends `"q"` and
waits up to 5s for the client process to exit; on this one run, out of ten,
the process did not exit within that window and was killed.

This matches the harness-side (not product-side) flake pattern already
documented for this suite: docs/reports/phase1-findings.md's task-033
finding ("the race is entirely in the test harness"; loosely-ordered
wall-clock proximity between a keypress and an out-of-process observation)
and docs/reports/phase2b1-findings.md's note of "a pre-existing, unrelated
flake ... a private tmux session lingering past its expected teardown, a
hook-timing race unrelated to [the feature under test]". Here the
observation racing against real time is the client's own process-exit
detection after a detail dialog was left open following a SIGKILL
reconcile — i.e. scheduler contention under the sibling container's shared
CPU (ten sequential full-suite runs, each spinning tmux + SQLite + a real
pty client) rather than a defect in deck's own §7 status/crash-capture
logic, none of which this scenario's own assertions faulted on. No product
code in internal/service, internal/store, internal/tmux or internal/tui
changed between run 3 (fail) and every other run (pass) — it is the same
binary under test throughout a single `ci/stability.sh` invocation.

This is recorded here as an honest 9/10 rather than papered over, per this
task's own successCriteria ("an honest 9/10 beats a hidden race"). No code
change is made in this task to chase a single non-reproduced 5s teardown
timeout; if it recurs at a measurable rate in a future stability run it
should be root-caused the way task 033 (phase1) root-caused its analogous
harness race.

